# Hardening headers applied to every response served by Cloudflare Pages.
# Keeps the site privacy-respectful and limits what can be abused.
#
# geolocation=(self) in the Permissions-Policy below: the map's locate-me
# button needs the API. Same-origin only and still permission-prompted by the
# browser. What that does and does not buy is set out honestly in the PRIVACY
# note at "4. LOCATE ME" in index.html — read that, not a summary here. In
# short: the page keeps the coordinate in memory, but moving the map to it
# does cause tile requests, and those now go to /tiles/* on this origin
# instead of a third party. Cloudflare, who already serve every request, can
# still see them. The claim is "no NEW party learns it", not "nobody does".
#
# script-src/style-src are 'self' only. Leaflet and Chart.js are vendored
# under /vendor/ rather than loaded from unpkg/jsdelivr: CDN copies handed
# those hosts every visitor's IP, and an unpinned bundle running in this
# origin could have read a visitor's location once geolocation was enabled.
# (Comment kept OUTSIDE the rule block on purpose: an unrecognised line inside
# a /* block risks being read as the end of it, which would silently drop
# every header after it.)

/*
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(self), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  # 'unsafe-inline' is required: no build step, so the app is inline script.
  # Every other source is 'self' — no third-party origin can load here.
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; script-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; font-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; connect-src 'self'; manifest-src 'self'

/api/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=60

/fonts/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
